      content: `<p>Building without a permit in Ghana is risky. Your structure can be demolished, you can be fined, and you will struggle to sell or rent the property. Here is exactly how to get your permit.</p><h2>Step 1: Prepare Your Documents</h2><p>You need: Site plan, architectural drawings, structural drawings, land title documents, environmental impact assessment (for larger projects), and tax clearance certificate.</p><h2>Step 2: Submit to the District Assembly</h2><p>Take your documents to the physical planning department of your district assembly. Pay the application fee (GHS 1,000–3,000 depending on location and house size).</p><h2>Step 3: Inspection and Approval</h2><p>The assembly will inspect your site within 2-4 weeks. If everything is in order, your permit is issued. Total timeline: 4-12 weeks.</p><h2>Cost Summary</h2><p>Application fee: GHS 1,000–3,000 | Inspection fee: GHS 500–2,000 | Plan registration: GHS 200–500 | Total: GHS 1,700–5,500</p><p>Factor permit costs into your total build budget using our calculator.</p>`,
