      slug: "rental-property-investment-ghana-2026",
      excerpt: "Ghana's housing deficit is 1.8 million units. How to build rental properties that generate 8-15% annual returns.",
      content: `<p>Ghana needs 1.8 million more housing units. This deficit creates a massive opportunity for rental property investors. Here is how to build for maximum ROI.</p><h2>Best Rental Locations</h2><p><strong>Student areas (Legon, Kumasi, Cape Coast):</strong> 10-15% returns. High demand, lower build costs, consistent occupancy.</p><p><strong>Urban middle-income (Spintex, Tema, Madina):</strong> 8-12% returns. Growing demand, good rental yields.</p><p><strong>Premium areas (East Legon, Airport):</strong> 5-8% returns. Lower yield but higher capital appreciation.</p><h2>Cost vs Return Analysis</h2><p>A 2-bedroom house costing GHS 350,000 to build can rent for GHS 1,500–3,000/month (GHS 18,000–36,000/year = 5-10% yield). A 4-unit compound at GHS 800,000 can generate GHS 6,000–12,000/month (GHS 72,000–144,000/year = 9-18% yield).</p><h2>Design for Rentals</h2><p>Separate entrances, individual water meters, durable finishes, low-maintenance materials.</p><p>Analyze your rental property ROI with our free cost calculator.</p>`,
