      content: `<p>Building in Ghana while living abroad is challenging but thousands of diaspora Africans do it successfully every year. This guide gives you everything you need to know before you send your first remittance.</p><h2>Step 1: Get Accurate Cost Estimates First</h2><p>Before you buy land or hire anyone, know exactly what your project will cost. Use our calculator to get a detailed estimate based on your specific requirements. This gives you a baseline that no contractor can inflate.</p><h2>Step 2: Choose Your Team Wisely</h2><p>You need people on the ground you can trust. Hire a project manager or quantity surveyor as your local representative. They inspect work, pay artisans, buy materials, and report to you weekly. This typically costs 5-10% of the build budget but saves you 20-30% in inflated costs and poor workmanship.</p><h2>Step 3: Legal Protection is Non-Negotiable</h2><p>Grant power of attorney to a trusted relative or lawyer. Sign detailed contracts with penalty clauses for delays. Use milestone-based payments — never pay more than 30% upfront. Escrow payments through a lawyer's account for major disbursements.</p><h2>Step 4: Use Technology to Stay Connected</h2><p>Require weekly video updates and site photos. Use WhatsApp groups for daily communication. Google Earth for site monitoring. Mobile money for small payments. Schedule visits during critical stages — foundation pouring, roofing, and handover.</p><h2>Common Diaspora Mistakes</h2><p>Trusting relatives to manage without construction experience. Sending too much cash at once. Not visiting during critical stages. Choosing contractors based solely on price. Not having a written contract.</p><p>Get your diaspora build estimate with our free calculator before you start.</p>`,
