      content: `<p>Land scams are the #1 problem diaspora investors face in Ghana. Here is how to buy land safely while living abroad — without losing your money to fraudsters.</p><h2>Never Buy Land Without a Local Lawyer</h2><p>Your lawyer is your most important partner. They verify ownership at the Lands Commission, check for encumbrances, review the title deed, and ensure proper transfer. Budget GHS 5,000–15,000 for legal fees. A good lawyer pays for themselves many times over.</p><h2>The Verification Process</h2><p>Step 1: Ask the seller for the original title documents. Step 2: Your lawyer conducts a search at the Lands Commission (GHS 500–1,000). Step 3: Verify the seller's identity matches the title. Step 4: Check for building permits and zoning restrictions. Step 5: Visit the physical site — have your local representative take photos and video.</p><h2>How Diaspora Buyers Get Scammed</h2><p>Fake land documents: GHS 50,000–500,000 lost. Multiple sales of the same plot: A common scam where fraudsters sell one plot to 5 different buyers. Family disputes: Land owned by a family where one member sells without consent. Government acquisition: Land that has been acquired by the government for public projects.</p><h2>Land Costs by Location (2026)</h2><p>East Legon: GHS 300,000–1,000,000 per plot. Kasoa: GHS 60,000–150,000. Kumasi: GHS 80,000–250,000. Takoradi: GHS 70,000–200,000. Emerging areas (Dodowa, Oyibi): GHS 40,000–100,000.</p><p>Include land costs in your total budget using our free calculator.</p>`,
