      content: `<p>Sending money from abroad to build in Ghana involves navigating exchange rates, transfer fees, and fraud risks. Here is how to get the most value from every cedi you send.</p><h2>Best Money Transfer Options</h2><p><strong>TransferWise (Wise):</strong> Best exchange rates, low fees, fast transfers. Send from 50+ countries. Typically 0.5-1% fee. Funds arrive in 1-2 business days.</p><p><strong>WorldRemit:</strong> Good for smaller transfers. Mobile money direct to Ghana. 1-3% fee depending on amount and destination.</p><p><strong>Western Union/MoneyGram:</strong> Widely available but more expensive (3-7% fees). Good for emergency payments.</p><p><strong>Cryptocurrency (USDT/USDC):</strong> Increasingly popular for large transfers. Near-zero fees, instant settlement. But requires both sender and receiver to have crypto wallets. Exchange to GHS via local P2P platforms.</p><h2>Tax Considerations for Diaspora Builders</h2><p>Ghana does not tax remittances sent for personal use or construction. However, rental income from the completed property IS taxable at 15% (withholding tax on rent). Consult a Ghana tax specialist for your specific situation.</p><h2>Recommended Payment Strategy</h2><p>Use Wise for regular material and labour payments. Use crypto for large contractor disbursements. Keep 20% of your budget in a Ghana bank account for emergency expenses. Never send the full project cost at once — use milestone-based payments.</p><p>Plan your diaspora build budget with our free calculator.</p>`,
