      content: `<p>Moving back to Ghana is exciting but complicated. From building your home to setting up your daily life, here is everything you need to plan for a smooth transition.</p><h2>Building vs Buying: Which is Better?</h2><p>Building gives you exactly what you want: custom design, quality materials, and the satisfaction of creating your own home. Timeline: 8-18 months. Cost: GHS 400,000–2,000,000+ depending on size and finish.</p><p>Buying an existing home is faster but you compromise on design. Timeline: 1-3 months. Cost: GHS 500,000–3,000,000+ in premium areas. Most existing homes need renovations costing GHS 50,000–200,000.</p><h2>Step 1: Secure Housing First</h2><p>Rent for 3-6 months while you search for land or a completed home. This gives you time to understand neighborhoods, commute times, and market prices without pressure. Budget GHS 15,000–60,000 per year for a good 3-bedroom rental.</p><h2>Step 2: Utilities and Services</h2><p>ECG (electricity): Connection fee GHS 500–2,000. GWCL (water): Connection fee GHS 500–1,500. Internet: GHS 300–800/month for fiber. Waste disposal: GHS 100–300/month.</p><h2>Step 3: Schools and Healthcare</h2><p>International schools: GHS 30,000–100,000 per year. Private hospitals: NHIS registration GHS 500/year plus private health insurance GHS 3,000–15,000/year.</p><p>Plan your return to Ghana with our free cost calculator.</p>`,
